<div class="exercise"><div class="ex-q"><p>Shorting the motor terminals (<span class="katex"><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.4306em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right:0.0359em;">v</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right:0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="katex-base"><span class="katex-strut" style="height:0.6444em;"></span><span class="mord">0</span></span></span></span>) slows the fall but does not stop it. Why?</p>
</div><details><summary>Show the solution</summary><div class="ex-s"><p>Back EMF drives a current that opposes relative motion between body and wheels, a damping torque. Damping removes energy but cannot create the restoring torque needed against gravity, so the unstable mode remains.</p>
</div></details></div>
